<h2>The provision in outline</h2>
<p>Section 146 BNSS operates on an existing order under Section 144 — whether for a wife, child or parent — and gives the Magistrate three powers: increase the allowance, reduce it, or cancel the order. Three triggers run through the provision: a change in the circumstances of the person receiving or paying; a decision of a competent civil court or Family Court bearing on the parties' rights; and, for a divorced wife, specific events — remarriage, or receipt of the sums payable on divorce under personal law, in which case cancellation follows the scheme of the section.</p>

<h2>Enhancement: the claimant's side</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cost of living:</strong> sustained inflation since the original order is a recognised ground; courts take judicial notice of it, though quantification still needs the claimant's updated expense record.</li>
  <li><strong>The payer's improved income:</strong> promotions, increments and business expansion are classic grounds — proved through salary slips summoned from the employer, income tax returns, and the asset-and-liability affidavits now standard in maintenance proceedings after Rajnesh v. Neha.</li>
  <li><strong>The child's trajectory:</strong> school fees become college fees; medical needs emerge. Orders for minor children are the most frequently enhanced category.</li>
  <li><strong>Drafting:</strong> plead the original order's factual basis, the specific changes since, and the revised need — a comparative table of then-versus-now expenses is far more persuasive than narrative.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Reduction and cancellation: the payer's side</h2>
<p>The payer's application succeeds or fails on the genuineness of the claimed reversal. Retirement at superannuation, documented business closure, or serious illness reducing earning capacity are legitimate grounds for reduction; resignation timed to the maintenance litigation, or income routed through relatives, is the pattern courts are quickest to see through — the settled principle that an able-bodied person is presumed capable of earning applies at the alteration stage as it does at the original hearing. Cancellation grounds are narrower and largely statutory: the recipient's remarriage, cohabitation inconsistent with the entitlement, mutual-consent separation arrangements that covered maintenance, or a civil court adjudication — for instance, a finding in matrimonial proceedings — that removes the foundation of the order.</p>

<h2>The interplay with civil court outcomes</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Maintenance order first</h3>
    <p>A later Family Court decree — divorce with permanent alimony under Section 25 HMA, or a restitution decree — is a changed circumstance. The Magistrate adjusts the Section 144 order to avoid double recovery and to reflect the civil adjudication.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Civil decision first</h3>
    <p>Where a competent civil court has already determined the parties' financial rights, the Section 144 order must be varied or cancelled in consequence, on application under Section 146 — the criminal-side order cannot run contrary to the civil adjudication.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Procedure and proof</h2>
<p>The application is a summary proceeding before the same jurisdiction that made the order. Both sides should file updated affidavits of assets and liabilities; the applicant bears the burden of proving the change, and the court can summon employer records, bank statements and tax filings. The order operates from the date the court specifies — alteration is not automatically retrospective — and until it is made, the existing order stands fully enforceable, including through the recovery machinery for arrears. Orders under the provision are themselves open to revision on the usual grounds.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Treat Section 146 BNSS as the scheduled maintenance review it is. Claimants with orders more than three or four years old, particularly for growing children, should audit the current adequacy of the amount against documented expenses. Payers facing genuine reversals should apply promptly rather than default — arrears accrued before an alteration application are rarely forgiven, and a record of voluntary compliance is the applicant's best credibility asset.</p>
</div>
